              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for canvassing, then the Latin for to pick out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: regō, regere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "regō, regere — \"to rule, to guide, to direct\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Rēx populum iūstē regēbat. The king ruled the people justly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "regō, regere, rēxī, rēctum. Rēctus, straight, is its participle; rēx is the man who rules. English regent and correct."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "regō is inherited from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to make straight, to direct\" (EtymDB), the same root as in rēx and rēctus (Lewis & Short). English regent and correct come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
